</li><li>편집인:권태호 </li><li>04186 6, Hyochangmok-gil, Mapo-gu, Seoul, Republic of Korea</li></ul>© Hankyoreh Media Group All Rights Reserved.</div></footer></div></div></div><script id=\"__NEXT_DATA__\" type=\"application/json\">{\"props\":{\"pageProps\":{\"label\":\"Business\",\"article\":{\"id\":1222053,\"displayType\":2,\"title\":\"South Korea shoots for the Stargate\",\"subtitle\":\"Many hope that the newly inked partnerships between Korean chipmakers and OpenAI will allow Korea to propel itself into a top-three AI powerhouse\",\"reporterList\":[],\"section\":{\"label\":\"English Edition\",\"code\":\"014000000\",\"name\":\"english_edition\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition\"},\"subSection\":{\"label\":\"Business\",\"code\":\"014003000\",\"name\":\"e_business\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_business\"},\"createDate\":\"2025-10-02 17:33\",\"updateDate\":\"2025-10-02 17:36\",\"content\":\"[%%IMAGE1%%]\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"text\\\"\\u003eSamsung and SK’s new partnership with OpenAI is significant in that Korean companies have now cemented themselves as essential allies of the US-led Stargate project, worth US$500 billion. Many observers see domestic firms’ assumption of the role of the main suppliers of artificial intelligence memory chips for the massive AI infrastructure project as a promising sign that Korea is well on its way to becoming one of the world’s top three AI powerhouses.\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eOpenAI signed letters of intent with SK Hynix and Samsung Electronics for memory supply on Wednesday. The projected high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supply volume, based on wafers, is up to 900,000 wafers per month — a number that is double the current global production capacity. The project will require Korea, a manufacturing powerhouse, and the US, a pioneer of AI technology, to work closely together. High-bandwidth memory chips are a core component used in AI accelerators that perform data learning for AI.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003e“Korea has all the ingredients to be a global leader in AI — incredible tech talent, world-class infrastructure, strong government support, and a thriving AI ecosystem,” OpenAI CEO Sam Altman stated during a meeting with President Lee Jae Myung.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003e“Samsung Electronics and SK Hynix plan to scale up production of advanced memory chips, targeting 900,000 DRAM wafer starts per month at an accelerated capacity rollout, critical for powering OpenAI’s advanced AI models,” OpenAI noted in a statement.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eSK Chairperson Chey Tae-won also stated, “We are honored to participate as a core partner in the Stargate project, aimed at building future global AI infrastructure. By leveraging SK’s integrated AI infrastructure capabilities from memory semiconductors to data centers, we will actively contribute to global AI infrastructure innovation and strengthen Korea’s AI competitiveness at a national level.”\\u003c/p\\u003e[%%IMAGE2%%]\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"text\\\"\\u003eSamsung Electronics similarly said it would “contribute toward meeting OpenAI’s memory demand with its extensive lineup of high-performance, energy-efficient DRAM solutions.”\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eSamsung and SK’s resolve to cooperate with OpenAI when constructing data centers and building AI services demonstrates how both companies are going beyond providing chips to AI companies to be active participants in the construction of an AI ecosystem.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eSK Telecom signed a memorandum of understanding with OpenAI, promising to join efforts in building an AI data center in South Jeolla Province. This marks a new collaborative effort following the SK Group’s groundbreaking ceremony in August with Amazon Web Services to build an AI data center in Ulsan.\\u0026nbsp;\\u003c/p\\u003e[%%IMAGE3%%]\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"text\\\"\\u003e\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eSamsung Group subsidiaries also signed letters of intent to jointly engage in the development of data centers and the provision of AI services. Samsung C\\u0026amp;T and Samsung Heavy Industries will collaborate with OpenAI to develop floating data centers — watertight structures offshore — with one expected to be based in Pohang, North Gyeongsang Province. These types of data centers are considered advantageous as they are not hindered by land scarcity, cost less to cool, and cut back on carbon emissions. Samsung SDS has pledged to strengthen cooperation in the design, construction, and operations of the data center.\\u003cbr\\u003e\\u0026nbsp;\\u003cbr\\u003eHowever, as the funds needed for the Stargate project are astronomical, some say it is too early to predict how profitable participating in this initiative will be. Reuters recently reported that OpenAI expects to lease, not buy, chips needed for its AI data centers.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"text\\\"\\u003eBy Park Jong-o, staff reporter\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp class=\\\"text\\\"\\u003ePlease direct questions or comments to [\\u003ca href=\\\"mailto:english@hani.co.kr\\\"\\u003eenglish@hani.co.kr\\u003c/a\\u003e]\\u003c/p\\u003e\",\"bgHeaderThumbnail\":\"\",\"imageList\":[{\"tag\":\"[%%IMAGE1%%]\",\"url\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/600/419/imgdb/original/2025/1002/9317593938181747.webp\",\"caption\":\"South Korean President Lee Jae Myung speaks with OpenAI CEO Sam Altman at the presidential office in Seoul on Oct. 1, 2025. (Yonhap)\",\"width\":600,\"height\":419,\"align\":\"center\",\"isZoom\":false},{\"tag\":\"[%%IMAGE2%%]\",\"url\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/970/647/imgdb/original/2025/1002/4717593938461519.webp\",\"caption\":\"Attendees at a reception for OpenAI CEO Sam Altman stand for a photo at the presidential office in Seoul on Oct. 1, 2025. Pictured are (left to right) Kim Yong-beom, chief policy director; Industry Minister Kim Jung-kwan; Samsung Electronics Chairman Lee Jae-yong; Sam Altman; President Lee Jae Myung; Chey Tae-won, chairman of SK Group; Bae Kyung-hoon, minister of science and ICT; and Kang Hoon-sik, Lee’s chief of staff. (courtesy of the presidential office)\",\"width\":970,\"height\":647,\"align\":\"center\",\"isZoom\":false},{\"tag\":\"[%%IMAGE3%%]\",\"url\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/900/589/imgdb/original/2025/1002/321759393874106.webp\",\"caption\":\"OpenAI CEO Sam Altman arrives in Korea at the Seoul Gimpo Business Aviation Center on Oct. 1, 2025. (Yonhap)\",\"width\":900,\"height\":589,\"align\":\"center\",\"isZoom\":false}],\"pictorialList\":[],\"pictorialCount\":0,\"mediaList\":[],\"image\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/600/419/imgdb/original/2025/1002/9317593938181747.jpg\",\"relatedArticleList\":[{\"id\":1222049,\"title\":\"Samsung, SK sign on to OpenAI’s Stargate initiative as strategic partners\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_business/1222049.html\",\"image\":{\"url\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/300/180/imgdb/original/2025/1002/541759393560722.jpg\",\"width\":300,\"height\":180},\"createDate\":\"2025-10-02\"}],\"tagList\":[],\"svcUrl\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_business/1222053.html\",\"commentCount\":0,\"hideComment\":false,\"originalUrl\":\"https://www.hani.co.kr/arti/economy/economy_general/1221891.html\"},\"editData\":[{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Editorial] Top priority in North Korean POW case should be human rights, not politics\",\"subtitle\":\"The guiding principle in handling this matter must be a humanitarian approach that respects the human rights of these two North Korean soldiers\",\"prologue\":\"Two North Korean prisoners of war captured by Ukrainian forces early last year recently arrived in South Korea.\\r\\n\\r\\nSeoul says it arranged for their entry in accordance with international law and humanitarian principles and decided to keep the process under wraps to protect the two men and their families.\\r\\n\\r\\nBut the main opposition People Power Party (PPP) is accusing the government of keeping the transfer secr\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279755.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0928/6117905733887763.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Column] Fixing the blurred border between South and North Korea\",\"subtitle\":\"Behind a recent incident in which a landmine exploded is a difference in perception between the two Koreas on the MDL\\\\'s location\",\"prologue\":\"Korea’s Military Demarcation Line (MDL) and Demilitarized Zone (DMZ) have been the focus of renewed attention since the explosion of a suspected landmine in the western DMZ on Sept. 21. Some are now claiming that the North Korean military broke through the armistice line’s barbed-wire fences to plant mines.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe MDL and the armistice line are one and the same. “Military Demarcation Line” is the official term that appears in the Armistice Agreement signed on July 27, 1953. People tend\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279765.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0928/8617905750498627.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Editorial] Shark sighting in Busan is a warning from nature\",\"subtitle\":\"Warming seas are bringing new wildlife to Korea’s surrounding waters\",\"prologue\":\"On Sept. 18, a copper shark swam into the artificial waterway at North Port Waterfront Park in central Busan.\\r\\n\\r\\nAfter the shark garnered nationwide attention, local governments, merchants, and the retail industry are welcoming this unexpected shark boom with open arms.\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279769.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0928/3117905756047539.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Column] Is AI about to destroy humanity?\",\"subtitle\":\"When people worry about a hostile takeover by AI, they are simply describing what a malevolent human has already done in the United States\",\"prologue\":\"In his celebrated short stories about robots, science fiction author Isaac Asimov devised three laws to prevent machines from overpowering humans. First of all, robots could not, under any circumstances, harm humans. Following from this prime directive, they had to follow human orders and also preserve their own existence. Asimov’s stories explored all the possible conflicts arising from the actual implementation of these laws.\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279772.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0928/3517905760119479.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Column] Harari’s 2030s: A new age of repressive desublimation\",\"subtitle\":\"\",\"prologue\":\"By Slavoj Žižek, Global Eminent Scholar at Kyung Hee University  \\r\\n In his short talk “By 2030, the World Will Be Unrecognizable,” Yuval Noah Harari presents his position on how the explosive developme\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279559.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0927/7317904739856884.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Guest essay] This Chuseok, think of the seasonal workers who plant and pick your food\",\"subtitle\":\"A Laotian seasonal worker shares his experience working on farms in Korea\",\"prologue\":\"By Lochana (alias), seasonal worker in Korea  \\r\\n I am a seasonal worker from Laos. Many of the Korean farmers I’ve worked with have affectionately referred to me as a “son” because of my knack for the\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1279556.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0927/771790472388276.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Editorial] To build back public confidence, Lee must address issues affecting everyday Koreans\",\"subtitle\":\"If he continues to maintain a humble and attentive attitude while also delivering tangible results on substantive policy issues, he may be able to regain the public’s trust and goodwill\",\"prologue\":\"Korean President Lee Jae Myung bowed his head during a press conference on Friday, apologizing for not “fully appreciating the unavoidable conflicts and pain that arise in the course of working to safeguard the people’s livelihoods, advancing reform, and building national unity.”\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1278840.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0921/6117899705533836.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Column] Korea-Japan cooperation still has a long way to go\",\"subtitle\":\"Despite optimism about the bilateral relationship, main points of tension and division remain\",\"prologue\":\"Last week, I attended a high-level strategic forum between Korea and Japan organized by the East Asia Foundation at Keio University in Tokyo. The meeting brought together politicians from both sides of the aisle, former bureaucrats, academics and experts for a vigorous debate about “the Northeast Asian order in a time of transition and new challenges for Korea-Japan cooperation.”\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1278833.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0921/6017899697539749.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"Trump has taken a wrecking ball to America’s democratic institutions\",\"subtitle\":\"America is repeating the mistakes it made a century ago in attempting to revive itself as an empire\",\"prologue\":\"Washington, DC, has been described as an “imperial capital,” and a walk through its center calls to mind the temple and plaza of ancient Rome. Most of the centers of power — the White House, the Capit\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_international/1278121.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/500/300/imgdb/original/2026/0916/8017895468789929.jpg\",\"width\":500,\"height\":300,\"imageType\":1}},{\"type\":\"article\",\"title\":\"[Column] Sending troops to Hormuz would be a fatal miscalculation by Seoul\",\"subtitle\":\"Under the present circumstances, a deployment to the Strait of Hormuz would be the worst imaginable decision: all cost and no benefit\",\"prologue\":\"By Pak Noja (Vladimir Tikhonov), professor of Korean studies at the University of Oslo  \\r\\n When I heard the Blue House was considering a military deployment to the Strait of Hormuz — and had even begun\",\"url\":\"/arti/english_edition/e_editorial/1278311.html\",\"isExternalLink\":false,\"image\":{\"imageUrl\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/228/137/imgdb/original/2026/0917/6117896301239482.jpg\",\"width\":228,\"height\":137,\"imageType\":1}}],\"mostReadArticleList\":[{\"title\":\"전체\",\"type\":\"all\",\"list\":[{\"id\":1280408,\"rank\":1,\"title\":\"Kyiv was first to request confidentiality in North Korean POW transfer, Seoul’s NIS says\",\"prologue\":\"\",\"image\":\"https://flexible.img.hani.co.kr/flexible/normal/640/382/imgdb/original/2026/1001/5717908342589432.jpg\",\"url\":\"